Huawei ring-buffer producer candidate
====================================
Parent: b6631cccfe0dd438aec88068bead52ffeff6d785
Reference: Pixel msm-4.14 bb17b7de4b570f2c0c32ddba0543cac03c58dd84

The original-author commits preceding the Huawei integration import ringbuf.c
in chronological history order, initially unwired. Their original messages and
trailers are retained. *.patch.txt records each exact donor file patch. Shared
verifier and mmap dependencies are separate candidates, not silently imported.

Supported core contract:
- Canonical map27, key/value0/0; power-of-two page-aligned capacity. Measured
  32768,4096,8192 capacities are supported. Generic capacities up to what the
  u32 API, record offset and size_t double-mapping bounds permit. Flags0 and
  optional NUMA_NODE; all other flags rejected. No map BTF association.
- Physical pages mapped twice for wraparound; old map.pages/precharge model
  charges unique pages, wrapper and page vector. Shared syscall layer performs
  actual charging/uncharge. Allocation/precharge failures free the wrapper.
- Reserve, commit/submit and discard core retain donor pending-position bound,
  BUSY/DISCARD ABI, serialized reservations, release publication, atomic commit,
  NMI trylock failure, wakeups and poll availability. A record is not consumable
  just because poll is readable: user must observe BUSY and reservation order.
- Pending scan uses != instead of < because monotonic unsigned counters can
  wrap. Host counterwrap regression fails on the exact donor comparison.
  The bounded scan assumes internal pending/producer counters delimit records
  created by reserve: validated aligned lengths, immutable headers except atomic
  BUSY/DISCARD transition, BUSY stops at the first uncommitted record. Userspace
  can write consumer_pos but cannot write pending/producer or record pages.
  Producer verification must exclude writes before/after each allocated record.
- Use old-tree POLLIN/POLLRDNORM (unsigned poll mask); newer donor EPOLL names
  are not supplied by this kernel's poll header.
- Old-tree map destruction waits synchronize_rcu, then irq_work_sync, before
  unmapping/freeing. Last fd alone does not mean last program/VMA reference.
- mmap callback permits writes only to offset0, one consumer page. All other
  mappings clear MAYWRITE; offset addition is checked before remapping.

Standalone boundary (before separately recorded integration):
- map_mmap/map_poll callbacks and struct slots exist, but generic file operations
  are owned by the separate mmap candidate. No claim of usable map-fd mmap yet.
- Every BPF helper access to map27 is rejected by the real verifier compatibility
  function. Reserve/commit core is unregistered; there is no unsafe producer
  helper exposure before acquired-reference and bounded-memory verification.
- Generic map lookup syscall rejects ringbuf before the older ERR_PTR-unsafe
  callback path. Update/delete/iteration callbacks return ENOTSUPP.
- No vmlinux BTF, function calls/JIT changes, socket routes or override added.

Integrated candidate:
- Shared mmap foundation e34bc301ab1ace1af2c1800a7c9067320edc5e69 and verifier
  ownership3f671f344a655633ea48de120724d505414ac266 are merged, preserving their
  history. Test-only cf84f4a44/5799f4c85 improve header and stack-spill coverage.
- Helpers131 reserve,132 submit,133 discard are registered in the actual
  net/core/filter.c bpf_base_func_proto path reached by CGROUP_SKB via
  sk_filter_func_proto. Registration is compiled only with BPF_SYSCALL, avoiding
  disabled-config symbol references. Other networking callers of this shared
  route also see these general producer helpers. Tracing routes are unchanged.
- Reserve requires a ringbuf map and constant allocation bound, returns nullable
  owned memory. Submit/discard require exact-base, nonnull live owned memory and
  release aliases before caller clobber. Map/helper compatibility is checked in
  both directions; submit/discard accept a reservation pointer, not a map.
- Helper130 output and134 query remain unsupported. Their ABI names and the
  preceding126-129 placeholders preserve numbering, not implementation.
- Only constant pointer arithmetic is supported by the ownership foundation;
  no unmeasured variable-offset or socket-pointer support is implied.
- Generic fd mmap/poll dispatch now invokes the ringbuf callback. Callback
  preserves file/VM-ops/private ownership and WRITE/MAYWRITE accounting on both
  success and failure. Only RO mappings clear MAYWRITE (the dispatcher does so
  first as well). File-held VMA refs remain a simulated host service in tests.

Validation: scripts/tests/test_ringbuf.py compiles the entire production core
and real verifier map/helper compatibility under ASan/UBSan. Allocation, page
ownership, VM service calls, RCU/IRQ and locks are explicit deterministic models.
POSIX memfd aliases supply real double-mapped byte storage for wraparound tests;
this does not execute kernel VMAs, page faults, SMP, IRQ or RCU. Failure injection
covers every allocation position and precharge; mutation controls test selected
invariants. Producer record cases use the measured32/8/12-byte payloads.
FD lifetime, VMA split/fork and helper-reference tests belong to shared foundation
suites and must be rerun after their integration. Full image and actual kernel
syscall/verifier/Android consumption remain separate gates.

Integration test scripts/tests/test_ringbuf_verifier.py runs actual network
prototype dispatch and extracted verifier call/NULL-branch/memory/stack logic.
Only helper backend functions are stubs that abort if executed. This is not
real BPF verifier program-load acceptance. Separate producer tests execute the
real helper bodies against modeled kernel services. Late compatibility failure
may already allocate verifier state; the error test discards it and checks
balanced cleanup rather than assuming failed check_call leaves state unchanged.

Huawei mmap correction: the older remap_vmalloc_range implementation resolves
the allocation after applying the offset. The callback therefore validates
offset and length against its own exposed position and doubled-data pages before
calling the remapper. Host tests extract the actual remapper functions with
modeled allocation/page insertion services, cover separate synthetic regions,
and reject invalid lengths/offsets before any page insertion. This validation
does not perform real kernel mapping or access device memory.

Reviewed mmap range correction (supersedes the initial overflow-only guard):
Huawei mm/vmalloc.c remap_vmalloc_range adds pgoff before find_vm_area. Its
bounds check applies to the allocation found at the translated address, not
necessarily the original ring. An A-fd offset could therefore select B's private
VM_USERMAP metadata. The first host shim incorrectly enforced original-region
bounds and masked this defect. The regression now extracts both actual remap
functions verbatim; only region lookup, page insertion and allocation remain
modeled. Two real POSIX-backed allocations reproduce the issue on172ee6766.
The Huawei adaptation validates nonempty page-aligned length, starting offset
and remaining length against this ring's2+2*data_pages exposed pages before
calling the remapper. Subtraction avoids offset+length overflow. Consumer-only
write and callback flags/file/VM-ops accounting remain unchanged. Foreign-region
and boundary tests, plus removal-of-range-check mutation, cover the correction.
